Skin cancer is a major public health issue, yet regular self-examinations and professional screenings are rarely done. Smartphone apps that use AI to assess skin lesions could help – but few people use them. In a project led by Susanne Gaube, we asked **what makes people choose an AI-enabled app or a dermatologist** for skin cancer screening.

#### What we did

We ran two online **forced-choice conjoint experiments** with participants from the United States (N = 1,591 and a replication with N = 308). Participants repeatedly chose between two screening offers that varied in provider type (AI-enabled app vs. dermatologist), costs, expertise, privacy policy, and the level of detail of the results. Afterwards, we assessed demographics, attitudes toward AI, and medical mistrust.

#### What we found

- The **type of provider** was the most important factor: participants chose the dermatologist more often than the app.
- **Costs, expertise, and privacy policy** also significantly influenced decisions.
- Preferences were rather consistent across age, gender, and ethnicity groups.
- People with **greater medical mistrust** were more inclined to choose the app.
- Trust, accuracy, and quality ratings were higher for the dermatologist – regardless of which provider was chosen.

#### What this means

AI-enabled screening apps will not replace the dermatologist in people's minds any time soon. Yet they may reach groups who distrust the medical system – which makes affordable, transparent, and privacy-respecting apps a potential way to close gaps in care for underserved communities.

#### Read the paper

Gaube, S., Biebl, I., Engelmann, M. K. M., **Kleine, A.-K.**, & Lermer, E. (2024). Comparing preferences for skin cancer screening: AI-enabled app vs dermatologist. *Social Science & Medicine, 349*, 116871. [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.socscimed.2024.116871](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.socscimed.2024.116871)
